    {
      "id": "xPymjgZLdk-hbXpGSQYvI",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1612.3584274223867,
      "y": 3366.622132758881,
      "width": 910.0891723632812,
      "height": 1325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "4JSsj-E38vJpFlsPkL4oh",
        "apF69k6e2oEFvixQMzY_r",
        "W68i1PrIiH2-GhGtMvKov",
        "rrIOX-jGEXQi4dSbd9vpb",
        "RUicMS2dvu_bRw5tfXjOs"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1rV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1239811047,
      "version": 4884,
      "versionNonce": 2126073316,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790711118767,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "LFM2 is a family of Liquid Foundation Models designed for efficient on-device deployment\nand strong task capabilities.\n\nThe LFM2 family covers 350M–8.3B parameters, including dense models (350M, 700M, 1.2B,\n2.6B) and a mixture-of-experts variant (8.3B total, 1.5B active), all with 32K context\nlength.\n\nLFM2-Audio-1.5B is built around the decoder-only LFM2-1.2B language backbone with\ndedicated components for audio input and output.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAudio Encoder\nAudio inputs are converted to LLM embeddings by an audio encoder followed by an MLP\nconnector. Raw 16 kHz waveforms are first transformed into 128-dimensional log-mel\nfeatures. A small stack of strided convolutions performs 8× temporal downsampling on the\nlog-mel features. Thus, the operates at a temporal resolution of 12.5 embeddings per second\nof input audio.\n\nAudio Decoder\nFor audio output, LFM2-Audio generates discrete audio codes. Specifically, the model\npredicts 8-codebook Mimi RVQ codes, which are later converted to a waveform by a\ndetokenizer. To keep inference latency low, they do not ask the LFM2 backbone to predict\nall eight codes in sequence. Instead, we use a much smaller RQ-Transformer as a decoder for\ncode generation.\n\nInterleaved Generation\nIn interleaved generation mode, LFM2-Audio generates the assistant response in both text\nand audio, with a predetermined interleaved pattern. This acts as a kind of chain-of-\nthought, where the model generates the semantic content in text, leveraging knowledge\nlearned during large-scale text-only pre-training, and subsequently translates the generated\ntext into audio. In our implementation, we generate 6 text tokens followed by 12 audio\ntokens and repeat until all text tokens are exhausted. \nWhen this happens, the model outputs a special <|end of text|> token, after which it\noutputs all remaining audio tokens until a special <|end of audio|> token is generated.\nThe 6 : 12 ratio of text and audio tokens is selected to keep text generation ahead of\naudio generation and to minimize time-to-first-audio token.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "LFM2 is a family of Liquid Foundation Models designed for efficient on-device deployment and strong task capabilities.\n\nThe LFM2 family covers 350M–8.3B parameters, including dense models (350M, 700M, 1.2B, 2.6B) and a mixture-of-experts variant (8.3B total, 1.5B active), all with 32K context length.\n\nLFM2-Audio-1.5B is built around the decoder-only LFM2-1.2B language backbone with dedicated components for audio input and output.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAudio Encoder\nAudio inputs are converted to LLM embeddings by an audio encoder followed by an MLP connector. Raw 16 kHz waveforms are first transformed into 128-dimensional log-mel features. A small stack of strided convolutions performs 8× temporal downsampling on the log-mel features. Thus, the operates at a temporal resolution of 12.5 embeddings per second of input audio.\n\nAudio Decoder\nFor audio output, LFM2-Audio generates discrete audio codes. Specifically, the model predicts 8-codebook Mimi RVQ codes, which are later converted to a waveform by a detokenizer. To keep inference latency low, they do not ask the LFM2 backbone to predict all eight codes in sequence. Instead, we use a much smaller RQ-Transformer as a decoder for code generation.\n\nInterleaved Generation\nIn interleaved generation mode, LFM2-Audio generates the assistant response in both text and audio, with a predetermined interleaved pattern. This acts as a kind of chain-of-thought, where the model generates the semantic content in text, leveraging knowledge learned during large-scale text-only pre-training, and subsequently translates the generated text into audio. In our implementation, we generate 6 text tokens followed by 12 audio tokens and repeat until all text tokens are exhausted. \nWhen this happens, the model outputs a special <|end of text|> token, after which it outputs all remaining audio tokens until a special <|end of audio|> token is generated.\nThe 6 : 12 ratio of text and audio tokens is selected to keep text generation ahead of audio generation and to minimize time-to-first-audio token.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
