    {
      "id": "6tz0GHrySjEmwrbXfUGqA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5412.910562131383,
      "y": 505.80863529099224,
      "width": 980.3690185546875,
      "height": 925,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "HuZJcmWA0FdAIcOD_r6Pb"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b25",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 848926394,
      "version": 5354,
      "versionNonce": 71915364,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790705154925,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Moshi introduces the first multi-stream audio language model, i.e. a model that explicitly processes \nthe input and output audio streams jointly. \n    --> This removes the concept of speaker turn but train the model on natural conversations with\narbitrary dynamics including overlap and interruptions. \n\nMoshi always listens and always generates sound, either speech or silence\n\nAt every time step, Moshi jointly handles three main streams:\n\n1. User audio stream: the user's speech is continuously encoded by Mimi into discrete semantic and\n        acoustic tokens.\n2. Moshi audio stream: in parallel, Moshi generates its own semantic and acoustic tokens.\n3. Inner Monologue text stream: Moshi also generates text tokens representing what it intends to\n        say.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe main components of Moshi are: \n    - a text language model backbone (Helium)\n    - a neural audio codec model (Mimi)\n    - hierarchical generation of semantic and acoustic (RQ Transformer)",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Moshi introduces the first multi-stream audio language model, i.e. a model that explicitly processes \nthe input and output audio streams jointly. \n    --> This removes the concept of speaker turn but train the model on natural conversations with \narbitrary dynamics including overlap and interruptions. \n\nMoshi always listens and always generates sound, either speech or silence\n\nAt every time step, Moshi jointly handles three main streams:\n\n1. User audio stream: the user's speech is continuously encoded by Mimi into discrete semantic and\n        acoustic tokens.\n2. Moshi audio stream: in parallel, Moshi generates its own semantic and acoustic tokens.\n3. Inner Monologue text stream: Moshi also generates text tokens representing what it intends to\n        say.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe main components of Moshi are: \n    - a text language model backbone (Helium)\n    - a neural audio codec model (Mimi)\n    - hierarchical generation of semantic and acoustic (RQ Transformer)",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": 1790444120208,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "chLJQkrEt5CQy0BFTQ---",
