    {
      "id": "34m_V8e0MH6QFJ1ghduK8",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5399.902390465708,
      "y": 1590.9946804382926,
      "width": 1026.9812554253476,
      "height": 925,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "0cSBCcIMmbANIbrUl5e-c"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b29",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1118718714,
      "version": 5745,
      "versionNonce": 475909732,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790705154925,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When dealing with streaming generating Q = 8 codebooks for each frame starts being a real\nproblem.\nAt a frame rate of 12.5Hz, 100 (12.5*8) autoregressive steps are needed for just one second \nof audio. Generating 100 tokens per second is unfeasable in most case scenarious. \nAs a comparison, text can be represented with around 3 to 4 tokens per second.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe Temporal Transformer always has S temporal steps rather than K * S, while the Depth Transformer\nperforms at most K steps for each temporal position.\n\n\nThe RQ-Transformer consists in two Transformer models,\n    - a larger Temporal Transformer, received the aggregated (sum) representation of the [K, S] \n        input sequence. This means it receves a sequence of S timesteps. The Temporal Transformer\n        produces a single temporal context embedding\n    - the smaller Depth Transformer operates along the codebook/depth dimension. Conditioned on the\n        temporal context, it autoregressively predicts the different token streams associated with the\n        current timestep.\n\nCompared with AudioLM which generates semantic tokens, then coarse acoustic tokens, then fine\nacoustic tokens, using separate stages, the RQ transformer generate them jointly with the acoustic\ntokens, which allows lower latency.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When dealing with streaming generating Q = 8 codebooks for each frame starts being a real\nproblem.\nAt a frame rate of 12.5Hz, 100 (12.5*8) autoregressive steps are needed for just one second \nof audio. Generating 100 tokens per second is unfeasable in most case scenarious. \nAs a comparison, text can be represented with around 3 to 4 tokens per second.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe Temporal Transformer always has S temporal steps rather than K * S, while the Depth Transformer\nperforms at most K steps for each temporal position.\n\n\nThe RQ-Transformer consists in two Transformer models,\n    - a larger Temporal Transformer, received the aggregated (sum) representation of the [K, S] \n        input sequence. This means it receves a sequence of S timesteps. The Temporal Transformer\n        produces a single temporal context embedding\n    - the smaller Depth Transformer operates along the codebook/depth dimension. Conditioned on the\n        temporal context, it autoregressively predicts the different token streams associated with the\n        current timestep.\n\nCompared with AudioLM which generates semantic tokens, then coarse acoustic tokens, then fine acoustic tokens, using separate stages, the RQ transformer generate them jointly with the acoustic tokens, which allows lower latency.\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": 1790499352175,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
