      "updated": 1790705154925,
      "created": 1790500638766,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Training Moshi",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Training Moshi",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "DTToQh1v4R0JtQhPmVYB7",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5442.42054302715,
      "y": 5877.771366026609,
      "width": 980.3690185546875,
      "height": 1975,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "8e3nSPJInu3CuMKsGpd-g"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2m",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 459402810,
      "version": 5861,
      "versionNonce": 535625564,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790705154925,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Moshi is trained in several stages rather than end-to-end from scratch.\n\n1. Train the audio codec, Mimi learns to compress speech into discrete audio tokens and\n        reconstruct the waveform. This gives Moshi its acoustic token representation.\n\n2. Pretrain the text backbone, Helium is first trained as a conventional text languageù\n        model. This supplies the linguistic knowledge and text-generation capability.\n\n3. Train the speech model on audio tokens. The model is then adapted to model multi-stream\n        sequences of Mimi tokens. They initialize the Temporal Transformer with Helium's weights,\n        while the Depth Transformer starts randomly initialized. They then train for 1 million steps\n        on roughly 7 million hours of audio, using initially one audio stream. More precisely, they\n        convert audio into Mimi token sequences and train Moshi autoregressively on those\n        sequences.\n\n4. Add and train the Inner Monologue. Moshi learns to predict text tokens before the\n        corresponding acoustic tokens:\n               text token → semantic/audio tokens\n               \n        They create the Inner Monologue targets from transcripts of Moshi’s own audio stream. The\n        procedure is:\n            1. Transcribe the audio with Whisper. Whisper provides both the transcript and word-level\n                    timestamps.\n            2. Tokenize the transcript with SentencePiece. Each spoken word may become one or\n                    several text tokens\n            3. Align those text tokens to Mimi’s frame rate. Mimi outputs audio tokens at 12.\n                    frames per second. That means the timeline is divided into 80 ms slots:\n\n                            Frame:      0       1       2       3       4       5\n                            Time:     0.00    0.08    0.16    0.24    0.32    0.40 s\n                            Audio:     A0      A1      A2      A3      A4      A5\n\n\n            4. Build a text stream synchronized with the audio stream using Whisper’s word-level\n                    timestamps. At the frame where a word starts, its SentencePiece tokens are\n                    inserted. For example, suppose Whisper gives:\n\n                                                \"hello\" starts at 1.20 s\n                                                \"there\" starts at 1.68 s\n            \n                    Since Mimi has one frame every 80 ms:\n\n                                                1.20 s  → frame 15\n                                                1.68 s  → frame 21\n\n                    Moshi places the text tokens for \"hello\" starting around frame 15. After those\n                    text tokens, it inserts PAD tokens until the next word begins:, and an EPAD token\n                    marks the transition before the next word.\n\n                            Time       t14      t15      t16      t17       t4      t5\n                            \n                            Text:      EPAD  \"Hello\"     PAD    EPAD   \"I\"      PAD\n                            Audio:      a0        a1      a2       a3        a4       a5\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n5. Train on two-speaker conversational data. They run speaker diarization with PyAnnote and both\n        speakers are encoded independently with Mimi, producing two sets of codec-token streams.\n        Those two sets are then presented to Moshi at the same time step, instead of\n        concatenating speakers temporally.\n        This is the stage that teaches it full-duplex behavior, including overlap, turn-taking,\n        interruptions, and backchannels.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Moshi is trained in several stages rather than end-to-end from scratch.\n\n1. Train the audio codec, Mimi learns to compress speech into discrete audio tokens and\n        reconstruct the waveform. This gives Moshi its acoustic token representation.\n\n2. Pretrain the text backbone, Helium is first trained as a conventional text languageù\n        model. This supplies the linguistic knowledge and text-generation capability.\n\n3. Train the speech model on audio tokens. The model is then adapted to model multi-stream\n        sequences of Mimi tokens. They initialize the Temporal Transformer with Helium's weights,\n        while the Depth Transformer starts randomly initialized. They then train for 1 million steps\n        on roughly 7 million hours of audio, using initially one audio stream. More precisely, they\n        convert audio into Mimi token sequences and train Moshi autoregressively on those\n        sequences.\n\n4. Add and train the Inner Monologue. Moshi learns to predict text tokens before the\n        corresponding acoustic tokens:\n               text token → semantic/audio tokens\n               \n        They create the Inner Monologue targets from transcripts of Moshi’s own audio stream. The\n        procedure is:\n            1. Transcribe the audio with Whisper. Whisper provides both the transcript and word-level\n                    timestamps.\n            2. Tokenize the transcript with SentencePiece. Each spoken word may become one or\n                    several text tokens\n            3. Align those text tokens to Mimi’s frame rate. Mimi outputs audio tokens at 12.\n                    frames per second. That means the timeline is divided into 80 ms slots:\n\n                            Frame:      0       1       2       3       4       5\n                            Time:     0.00    0.08    0.16    0.24    0.32    0.40 s\n                            Audio:     A0      A1      A2      A3      A4      A5\n\n\n            4. Build a text stream synchronized with the audio stream using Whisper’s word-level\n                    timestamps. At the frame where a word starts, its SentencePiece tokens are\n                    inserted. For example, suppose Whisper gives:\n\n                                                \"hello\" starts at 1.20 s\n                                                \"there\" starts at 1.68 s\n            \n                    Since Mimi has one frame every 80 ms:\n\n                                                1.20 s  → frame 15\n                                                1.68 s  → frame 21\n\n                    Moshi places the text tokens for \"hello\" starting around frame 15. After those\n                    text tokens, it inserts PAD tokens until the next word begins:, and an EPAD token\n                    marks the transition before the next word.\n\n                            Time       t14      t15      t16      t17       t4      t5\n                            \n                            Text:      EPAD  \"Hello\"     PAD    EPAD   \"I\"      PAD\n                            Audio:      a0        a1      a2       a3        a4       a5\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n5. Train on two-speaker conversational data. They run speaker diarization with PyAnnote and both\n        speakers are encoded independently with Mimi, producing two sets of codec-token streams.\n        Those two sets are then presented to Moshi at the same time step, instead of\n        concatenating speakers temporally.\n        This is the stage that teaches it full-duplex behavior, including overlap, turn-taking,\n        interruptions, and backchannels.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": 1790502407061,
