      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790709433523,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Full-Duplex for Speech, Language, and Action",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Full-Duplex for Speech, Language, and Action",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "created": 1790705708011,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "sn92_Ql8TV5HvtieMakqh",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 8750.139765629285,
      "y": 1583.7456982887888,
      "width": 980.3690185546875,
      "height": 3125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "JouFrcxsjCLYJFKmLL0Tt",
        "qyeb00i6zaeTugxOMtobf",
        "X3WcN5KujgvgB0CxVGweC",
        "gXP-WWcUBkH6H40qPYn-S"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3b",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1173982172,
      "version": 6031,
      "versionNonce": 643410148,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790709433523,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Existing duplex backbones still lack a native channel for in-conversation planning and tool calling,\nleaving real-time agentic behaviour either tied to turn boundaries or relegated to an external\ncascade.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis causes two problems\n    1) simple VAD can't tell a thinking pause from the end of a sentence, or a quick \"mm-hmm\" from a\n        real interruption.\n    3) Semantic VAD remains a separate detector in front of the pipeline\n    2) tool calls don't fit well into this turn-by-turn loop: they either delay the answer\n\n\nDuplexSLA, a native full-duplex Speech–Language–Action foundation model that handles three\nchannels together:\n    - The user channel is the incoming audio from the person, which the model only listens to. \n    - The assistant channel is the model's own speech. \n    - The action channel is a hidden text stream for decisions, reasoning and tool calls.\nAll three share the same clock, listening, speaking and acting happen side by side instead of taking\nturns.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDuplexSLA splits the conversation into small time slices of 160 milliseconds. Every 160 ms, the model\nproduces two things at the same time:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1. Assistant speech (always). Five tokens per chunk, the \"TA4\" unit:\n    - 1 text anchor token: the word or syllable being spoken, or a special token meaning \"I'm silent\"\n        (<vad_silence>) or \"padding\" (<tts_pad>)\n    - 4 discrete audio tokens, each covering 40 ms of sound\n\n2. Action text (0 to 10 tokens per 160 ms chunk). A separate text stream that never gets spoken\naloud. It can contain:\n    - Turn-taking labels: response (\"user finished speaking\"), interrupt (\"user interruption detected\"),\n        backchannel (\"agreeing tone detected\")\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    - Planning text: a short internal thought, e.g. \"The user feels cold, I should turn on the AC\".\n        Like \"chain-of-thought\" in text LLMs, writing the intent first (\"user is cold → needs heat\")\n        helps the model pick the right function and arguments rather than jumping straight to JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n    - Tool calls: JSON commands that an external system executes, e.g.\n        <|toolcall_begin|>{\"function\": \"set_car_setting\", \"arguments\": \"AC=26°\"}<|toolcall_end|>\n        Two patterns are particularly important:\n\n            Backchannel-triggered tool calling. A short user utterance that is topically unrelated to\n            the current dialogue but triggers a tool call.\n            Multi-action tool calling. A single user request can spawn several tool calls, the calls are\n            emitted in semantic order along the user’s request, and the assistant audio runs in\n            parallel with each call’s planning text.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nEach chunk ends with <|action_end|>, even if the action stream was empty.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Existing duplex backbones still lack a native channel for in-conversation planning and tool calling, leaving real-time agentic behaviour either tied to turn boundaries or relegated to an external cascade.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis causes two problems\n    1) simple VAD can't tell a thinking pause from the end of a sentence, or a quick \"mm-hmm\" from a\n        real interruption.\n    3) Semantic VAD remains a separate detector in front of the pipeline\n    2) tool calls don't fit well into this turn-by-turn loop: they either delay the answer\n\n\nDuplexSLA, a native full-duplex Speech–Language–Action foundation model that handles three channels together:\n    - The user channel is the incoming audio from the person, which the model only listens to. \n    - The assistant channel is the model's own speech. \n    - The action channel is a hidden text stream for decisions, reasoning and tool calls.\nAll three share the same clock, listening, speaking and acting happen side by side instead of taking turns.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDuplexSLA splits the conversation into small time slices of 160 milliseconds. Every 160 ms, the model produces two things at the same time:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1. Assistant speech (always). Five tokens per chunk, the \"TA4\" unit:\n    - 1 text anchor token: the word or syllable being spoken, or a special token meaning \"I'm silent\"\n        (<vad_silence>) or \"padding\" (<tts_pad>)\n    - 4 discrete audio tokens, each covering 40 ms of sound\n\n2. Action text (0 to 10 tokens per 160 ms chunk). A separate text stream that never gets spoken aloud. It can contain:\n    - Turn-taking labels: response (\"user finished speaking\"), interrupt (\"user interruption detected\"),\n        backchannel (\"agreeing tone detected\")\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    - Planning text: a short internal thought, e.g. \"The user feels cold, I should turn on the AC\".\n        Like \"chain-of-thought\" in text LLMs, writing the intent first (\"user is cold → needs heat\")\n        helps the model pick the right function and arguments rather than jumping straight to JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n    - Tool calls: JSON commands that an external system executes, e.g.\n        <|toolcall_begin|>{\"function\": \"set_car_setting\", \"arguments\": \"AC=26°\"}<|toolcall_end|>\n        Two patterns are particularly important:\n\n            Backchannel-triggered tool calling. A short user utterance that is topically unrelated to\n            the current dialogue but triggers a tool call.\n            Multi-action tool calling. A single user request can spawn several tool calls, the calls are\n            emitted in semantic order along the user’s request, and the assistant audio runs in\n            parallel with each call’s planning text.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nEach chunk ends with <|action_end|>, even if the action stream was empty.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": 1790705708011,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
